.TH OPMLAUNCHINFO "1" "November 2026" "opmlaunchinfo" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
opmlaunchinfo \- print what an OPM deck needs to be launched
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B opmlaunchinfo
\fIdeck\fR
.br
.B opmlaunchinfo
\fB\-h\fR | \fB\-\-help\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B opmlaunchinfo
reads \fIdeck\fR as the Flow of the same OPM version reads it, and prints on
standard output, as JSON, what is needed to launch it. Today that is the
slaves of a reservoir coupling master: a deck with the \fBSLAVES\fR keyword
makes Flow start one more simulation per slave, with the processes the deck
gives it (\fBNUM_PE\fR), and the job must have room for all of them.
.PP
It is mostly run by
.BR runflow (1),
which needs that number before it starts or submits a run, but it can be run
by hand to see what a deck asks for.
.SH READING THE DECK
\fIdeck\fR is the deck file, e.g. \fBMASTER.DATA\fR; files it includes are
found relative to it, as Flow finds them.
.PP
It is read with the parser of the opm\-common it was built with, with Flow's
default settings (not strict): a deck that Flow of this version reads, it
reads too, and it refuses what Flow refuses in \fBSLAVES\fR. Each OPM
installation has its own \fBopmlaunchinfo\fR, so that a deck is read as the
Flow it will run with reads it.
.PP
Only the RUNSPEC and SCHEDULE sections are read when the deck has its section
keywords (RUNSPEC, GRID, PROPS, SOLUTION, SCHEDULE) in the deck file itself,
in the usual order; then large grid files are not opened, and even a full
field model is read in well under a second. Otherwise, e.g. when SCHEDULE is
in an included file, the whole deck is read.
.SH OUTPUT
One JSON object:
.PP
.RS
.nf
{ "schema": 1,
  "slaves": [ { "name": "RES\-1", "deck": "RC\-01_MOD1_HIST",
                "directory": "../mod1", "num_pe": 4 },
              { "name": "RES\-2", "deck": "RC\-01_MOD2_HIST",
                "directory": "../mod2", "num_pe": 1 } ] }
.fi
.RE
.TP
\fBschema\fR (integer)
The version of this format, now \fB1\fR (see COMPATIBILITY).
.TP
\fBslaves\fR (list)
One object per slave of \fBSLAVES\fR, in the deck's order; empty for a deck
without \fBSLAVES\fR, which is not a coupling master. A name given twice
counts once, with its last record, as in Flow.
.RS
.TP
\fBname\fR (string)
the slave's name (item 1, SLAVE_RESERVOIR);
.TP
\fBdeck\fR (string)
its deck, without \fB.DATA\fR (item 2, SLAVE_ECLBASE);
.TP
\fBdirectory\fR (string)
the directory of its deck, as written in the deck (item 4, DIRECTORY), e.g.
\fB../mod1\fR; Flow takes it relative to the directory the master runs in;
.TP
\fBnum_pe\fR (integer)
the processes Flow starts it with (item 5, NUM_PE; 1 if defaulted).
.RE
.PP
The slots a coupled run needs are its own processes plus the sum of
\fBnum_pe\fR: \fB1 + 4 + 1 = 6\fR for a one\-process master with the slaves
above.
.SH COMPATIBILITY
The output is a contract between versions: a \fBrunflow\fR of one OPM version
reads the output of another version's \fBopmlaunchinfo\fR. So members are only
ever added to schema 1, never changed or removed; a change of meaning gets a
new \fBschema\fR number. Programs that read the output must ignore members they
do not know, and refuse a \fBschema\fR newer than they know.
.SH HOW RUNFLOW USES IT
For each run,
.BR runflow (1)
looks for the \fBopmlaunchinfo\fR of the Flow version it runs, in this order:
.IP 1. 4
the version's \fBlaunchinfo\fR in \fBrunflow.json\fR (an error if it cannot be
run);
.IP 2. 4
\fBopmlaunchinfo\fR in the same directory as that version's \fBflow\fR, as an
installation has them;
.IP 3. 4
none: runflow reads the deck with its own parser, which is a newer one for
versions released before \fBopmlaunchinfo\fR existed.
.PP
\fBflowversions show\fR
.IR version
names the one found. In a build tree, \fBflow\fR and \fBopmlaunchinfo\fR are
in different directories (opm\-simulators', opm\-common's), so a build is
given its \fBlaunchinfo\fR in the configuration.
.SH EXAMPLES
A coupling master:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ opmlaunchinfo RC\-01_MAST_HIST.DATA
{
	"schema":	1,
	"slaves":	[{
			"name":	"RES\-1",
			"deck":	"RC\-01_MOD1_HIST",
			"directory":	"../mod1",
			"num_pe":	4
		}, {
			"name":	"RES\-2",
			"deck":	"RC\-01_MOD2_HIST",
			"directory":	"../mod2",
			"num_pe":	1
		}]
}
.fi
.RE
.PP
Any other deck:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ opmlaunchinfo SPE1CASE2.DATA
{
	"schema":	1,
	"slaves":	[]
}
.fi
.RE
.PP
The total processes of the slaves, in a script:
.PP
.RS
.nf
opmlaunchinfo MASTER.DATA | python3 \-c \(aqimport json, sys
print(sum(s["num_pe"] for s in json.load(sys.stdin)["slaves"]))\(aq
.fi
.RE
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
Errors go to standard error, as \fBopmlaunchinfo:\fR \fImessage\fR, and
nothing is printed on standard output:
.TP
\fBcannot read\fR \fIdeck\fR\fB:\fR \fIreason\fR
The deck, or a file it includes, does not exist or cannot be read, or the
parser refuses it; the reason is the parser's.
.TP
\fBthe SLAVES keyword can only be given once in the schedule\fR
.TQ
\fBSLAVES\fR \fIname\fR\fB: the number of processors must be positive\fR
What Flow also refuses.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 when the information is printed; 1 if the deck cannot be read or Flow would
refuse its \fBSLAVES\fR; 2 for wrong arguments.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR runflow (1),
.BR flowversions (1)
